  "structure": {
    "block_count": 64,
    "blocks": [
      {
        "block_id": "block_000000",
        "block_index": 0,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h1",
        "text": "Toddler Lunch Ideas",
        "text_sha256": "585ba8107c7147e94f21f856c363078f9d21d4dd1db7a564e39181d3714da989",
        "word_count": 3
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000001",
        "block_index": 1,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Toddler lunch ideas",
        "text_sha256": "dddc798dfa6bbab4b82e40e45fdc8aedbca9a5338e4876739fe5072b0a16570f",
        "word_count": 3
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000002",
        "block_index": 2,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Put PB&Js and chicken nuggets to rest with these creative, nutritious and kid-friendly lunch ideas.",
        "text_sha256": "6909c28752e9e06346d10e1058e15755691450fd4ec992f79ed461e5c900b4bb",
        "word_count": 15
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000003",
        "block_index": 3,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Some days, feeding yourself the right midday meal can be a challenge. Making lunch for a probably-picky growing toddler who needs the right nutrients and might have dietary restrictions, too (and even if they don’t, might attend a school where certain foods are banned)? Downright daunting.",
        "text_sha256": "d59b2e4b69782798a1162dbdc5ade77355e5f903d280aaac32381bbdbe736ddd",
        "word_count": 46
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000004",
        "block_index": 4,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "It doesn’t need to be. Toddlers — children who are between 12 and 35 months — can eat most whole foods that adults can eat, just in modified, safe-to-chew textures. Here's what parents need to know when making their toddler's lunch.",
        "text_sha256": "3201f6fe4b32086cccc8efb8449b80efcf6b05b46cb42e3724b1d70f69102514",
        "word_count": 41
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000005",
        "block_index": 5,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Tips for making healthy lunches for toddlers",
        "text_sha256": "da2adbe040dafa63636768bcd92e1faf72b48c8512ac24ec666db89eba7d9a6d",
        "word_count": 7
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000006",
        "block_index": 6,
        "block_type": "li",
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "Keep portion sizes in mind. A toddler's tummy is still tiny, notes Julie Stefanski, R.DN., a spokesperson for the Academy of Nutrition and Dietetics. “A serving size for a toddler is only about 25 percent to 50 percent of an adult portion,” she says.",
        "text_sha256": "0003775b71368ea70bb0b3876c0e8f5b99dc768ed359568cd8bbc82643c2bfa9",
        "word_count": 44
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000007",
        "block_index": 7,
        "block_type": "li",
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "Don't be deterred by picky eating. Just because a child doesn’t try a food the first (or third) time they see it doesn’t mean they won't eventually like it. “It may take 15 times for a toddler to see a new food before they even try it,” says Stefanski. “Even if they don’t try it, you’ve met the goal of exposing them to the new food.”",
        "text_sha256": "b49df112cfa236910b5383807272e4df7c2fd23e95a2256b5de259a1911a2788",
        "word_count": 66
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000008",
        "block_index": 8,
        "block_type": "li",
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "Alternate foods. Another strategy to combat picky eating is alternating foods to expose kids to more variety. For example, if your toddler is into sandwiches, alternate the bread between whole-wheat bread, whole-grain English muffins, whole-wheat pita and whole-wheat wraps, then alternate spreads between cream cheese, nut or seed butter, avocado and hummus.",
        "text_sha256": "7ed2b3fc45511bf370db30e2882776bf37aef6fb7349fb603e7a6f36d85f26b7",
        "word_count": 52
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000009",
        "block_index": 9,
        "block_type": "li",
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "Frozen produce is your friend. Only have a few minutes to whip up a lunch for your little one? “Using frozen produce will save busy parents lots of time, since it is prepped and ready to cook, and is just as nutritious as fresh produce found in the produce aisle,” says pediatric dietitian Nicole Silber, R.D. Canned beans, vegetables and fish can also make lunch prep a breeze.",
        "text_sha256": "afdc598d64a214815a4bafc9e53f7522a996483c1e24be93fc0fccdb8fb75268",
        "word_count": 68
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000010",
        "block_index": 10,
        "block_type": "li",
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "Avoid unsafe and unhealthy foods. Foods toddlers should steer clear of include those with added sugar and any possible choking hazards; the American Academy of Pediatrics (AAP) recommends avoiding popcorn, hot dogs (unless cut into small pieces), sticky peanut butter, marshmallows, raw carrots or celery and any round shapes like whole cherry tomatoes, whole grapes, seeds or hard candies for this reason. Foods like meat, raw fruits or vegetables and potatoes should always be cut up into small, bite-sized pieces, no larger than one-half inch.",
        "text_sha256": "0b396062166931d95d02fc07198d798c91c6880c667dce80f72f59152834a487",
        "word_count": 85
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000011",
        "block_index": 11,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h2",
        "text": "Best healthy toddler lunch ideas",
        "text_sha256": "58cd786942e0aad14439965fbb65e25dca803a5519569499a4ef81be26653682",
        "word_count": 5
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000012",
        "block_index": 12,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Try these lunch box ideas from Stefanski, Silber and Sophia DeSantis of the blog Veggies Don’t Bite .",
        "text_sha256": "6a54e8238b1b8e356fe87eef77d8762361a1f87016853aef108a79195e5067f6",
        "word_count": 18
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000013",
        "block_index": 13,
        "block_type": "heading",
        "tag": "h3",
        "text": "Best toddler lunch ideas for day care",
        "text_sha256": "0ef2a506e36b76b39eb4e6b302f939b4a63fbdf2e100fe5854eb6258199b4a42",
        "word_count": 7
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000014",
        "block_index": 14,
        "block_type": "p",
        "tag": "p",
        "text": "Check with your child’s day care manager to see if you need to avoid certain food allergens (the most common are dairy, wheat, fish, shellfish, eggs, soy, peanuts and tree nuts), but unless your child has a true allergy, the AAP does not recommend limiting these foods.",
        "text_sha256": "fb2fe87ffe47e9c1e4047c65e8e0a3d05e0758a37128e20083eefff22dd48218",
        "word_count": 47
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000015",
        "block_index": 15,
        "block_type": "li",
        "tag": "li",
        "text": "Lentil and veggie stir fry. Sauté lentils and dice veggies the night before and serve with brown rice.",
        "text_sha256": "942280671e74fe3c86138727998008200ece18a08d133558967005cc2ad63ed9",
        "word_count": 18
      },
      {
        "block_id": "block_000016",
